00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
trans athletes and allies are fighting a  surge of proposed anti-transgender  legislation  across the u.s many of these bills have  aimed to ban  trans athletes from competing on teams  that affirm their gender identities  trans girls and trans women are the main  targets of these bills with backers  believing they have an unfair advantage  over  cisgender competitors but trans rights  advocates say  it's not about wins and losses lindsay  hecox's dream of running cross-country  for boise state  was put on hold when idaho passed house  bill 500  in the spring of 2020. billie jean king  candace parker  megan repino and almost 200 other  athletes signed an  amicus brief speaking out against the  law which has since been suspended  this isn't the first time the sports  world has pushed back against anti-trans  legislation  in 2016 the nba and ncaa  pulled major events out of north  carolina in response to a bill  prohibiting trans people from using  public restrooms that match their  identity this contributed to the law's  partial repeal  in 2017. but athletes like cece telfer  the first out trans woman to win an ncaa  title  want to see the organization take a  firmer stance on current legislation  as a trans athlete uh first of all i'm  not a trump i'm not a threat to the prom  woman's sport  because i am a woman the ncaa has since  stated host sites must be  free of discrimination trans students  will also now be protected under title  ix the surge of legislation is  unprecedented  but discrimination against trans  non-binary and intersex  people is not new pride month  historically celebrates and honors  lgbtq plus communities but activism  against anti-trans legislation reminds  us of where pride began  in struggle and for visibility power  solidarity and equality
 

